What you looking at?

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:11.083
Go back down.

00:25:11.083 --> 00:25:14.360
She's like, I've never
seen things from this view.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:16.000
Mythical.com.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:16.833
Okay.

00:25:16.833 --> 00:25:17.666
Were we still going?

00:25:17.666 --> 00:25:19.660
That was a very pregnant pause.

00:25:19.660 --> 00:25:21.303
Yeah, had to push it out.

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:25.690
You actually thought
of talking about space?

00:25:25.690 --> 00:25:26.940
Why did you do that?

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:29.120
What made you think about spaces?

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:29.953
Well...

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.690
Me and Lincoln building the thing?

00:25:33.650 --> 00:25:36.175
Well, no, it was the fact
that we were coming back.

00:25:36.175 --> 00:25:37.330
It was twofold.

00:25:37.330 --> 00:25:40.130
Yeah, what we talked about,
but what's the other fold?

00:25:41.760 --> 00:25:44.163
It was that yesterday.

00:25:46.190 --> 00:25:47.430
Was that yesterday?
Yeah.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:48.263
We were over in the creative house.

00:25:48.263 --> 00:25:49.780
And the creative house,

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:51.400
what the vision for the creative house

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:54.700
of being decorated and moved in.

00:25:54.700 --> 00:25:58.980
Of course, the virus also
infected that process.

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:01.130
Yeah it did.
Really slowed it down.

00:26:01.130 --> 00:26:05.510
But furniture for different
parts of the house,

00:26:05.510 --> 00:26:06.343
including the living room,

00:26:06.343 --> 00:26:08.780
and then some stuff in
your office came in.

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:12.730
And Link and Lincoln were
putting some of it together.

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:17.720
And so I was just
thinking about, it's funny

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:21.573
because we designed this
space here for ourselves.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:27.180
I mean, we didn't design this space

00:26:27.180 --> 00:26:28.460
that we're in right now, necessarily.

00:26:28.460 --> 00:26:29.920
We took advantage of the
fact that it was like

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:31.530
an awesome recording studio

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:34.050
that happened to be here at
the space we were renting.

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:36.993
But we designed our space for our office.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:42.470
And then, we've talked about this already,

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:43.990
but how we came to the conclusion

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:46.740
that we needed our own space again,

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:51.740
ironically, because this
space doesn't necessarily say,

00:26:52.817 --> 00:26:54.730
"Hey, Rhett and Link, create."

00:26:54.730 --> 00:26:55.987
It says "Hey, Rhett and Link."

00:26:55.987 --> 00:26:57.477
"Have meetings.

00:26:57.477 --> 00:26:58.887
"You're business boys."

00:26:58.887 --> 00:27:01.990
"Supervise and be business boys."

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:05.850
And so giving this
other space where it was

00:27:06.920 --> 00:27:10.300
a new zone where we
could express ourselves.

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:14.340
And we knew that that first
meant setting it up it.

00:27:16.373 --> 00:27:21.373
Because we have a long
history of setting up spaces.

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:25.140
I mean I'll go all the
way back to college.

00:27:25.140 --> 00:27:28.490
We really started to appreciate
having our own space.

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:31.180
I mean that, remember how exciting it was

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:36.070
when we moved in to our
dorm room at NC State.

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:39.610
And I remember the first
few weeks of school

00:27:39.610 --> 00:27:42.530
you're walking around the quad
and you're going to classes.

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:43.640
And do you remember this?

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:46.590
All of these vendors had set up.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:50.530
They were selling crap, mostly posters.

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:51.610
You remember this?

00:27:51.610 --> 00:27:55.330
They were selling posters
all over the quad.

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:58.930
And there were huge
posters, little posters.

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:03.030
Mostly just band posters
and cool movie poster.

00:28:03.030 --> 00:28:05.170
And they knew that college
students who were just

00:28:05.170 --> 00:28:08.710
moving into their dorm
rooms would just go bananas.

00:28:08.710 --> 00:28:11.380
Oh yeah, it's like, oh, Bob Marley.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.800
So many Bob Marley posters.

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:16.150
I kind of felt like I needed
to get a Bob Marley poster

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:17.670
just because it seemed
like the thing to do.

00:28:17.670 --> 00:28:22.370
But instead we got a pulp fiction poster

00:28:22.370 --> 00:28:25.760
where Vincent Vega and--
Jules.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:30.190
Jules were, I think it's
after they were shot at

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:32.430
and miraculously didn't get hit

00:28:32.430 --> 00:28:33.650
and they just opened fire.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:34.840
Yeah, the scene with Brad.

00:28:34.840 --> 00:28:39.840
And then we also bought a
meeting, a poster of a meeting

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:44.947
between Elvis, who seemed to be in his--

00:28:45.830 --> 00:28:46.670
Last days.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:49.810
On his last legs, meeting President Nixon.

00:28:49.810 --> 00:28:52.593
And I actually don't know
the story behind that poster.

00:28:52.593 --> 00:28:54.900
Well the story behind that poster is--

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:56.740
I just thought, okay, it's kind of cool.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:29:00.880
No, it was a, I'm gonna get
some of these details wrong.

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:03.480
But my understanding is that

00:29:06.623 --> 00:29:08.810
it was an anti-drug meeting.

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:10.590
That's what makes it so funny.

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:11.787
It was an ironic poster

00:29:11.787 --> 00:29:14.160
And I think that they
may have even made Elvis

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:16.940
like an honorary DEA officer or something.

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.040
I don't know how the legend goes,

00:29:19.040 --> 00:29:22.700
but they had been talking about drugs.

00:29:22.700 --> 00:29:24.650
And of course, Elvis obviously

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:27.023
had been using a lot of drugs.

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:31.700
And you've got Nixon in his
suit and Elvis in his suit,

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:33.490
which is a very different kind of suit.

00:29:33.490 --> 00:29:35.030
Yeah.
We loved that poster, man.

00:29:35.030 --> 00:29:37.505
Yeah, and I didn't even know
the full story behind it.

00:29:37.505 --> 00:29:39.860
And I just thought it was odd and ironic.

00:29:39.860 --> 00:29:41.070
I could sense the irony.

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:43.493
I didn't have to, it didn't
have to be explained to me.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:49.268
That's where we got Mr
Fly from the thrift store,

00:29:49.268 --> 00:29:54.268
The velour couch that then
we would pose like Lionel,

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:55.890
and made project Lionel.

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:58.560
And then we decorated the
wall with pictures of Lionel.

00:29:58.560 --> 00:30:00.040
I mean, if you don't know the story,

00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:01.523
you can get it in the book of Mythicality.

00:30:01.523 --> 00:30:06.030
But it was just so exciting
to have this space,

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:08.200
this hideout, this little dungeon space.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:09.610
I mean, we had no light coming in,

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:13.000
our window was right under
the steps to the first floor.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:15.115
And it was pitch dark
in the broad daylight.

00:30:15.115 --> 00:30:17.590
All the time, which is good for college

00:30:17.590 --> 00:30:19.677
because you can just
sleep in the middle of day

00:30:19.677 --> 00:30:20.577
and it feels like night.

00:30:20.577 --> 00:30:22.460
And it smelled like a basement.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:23.437
I like that smell.

00:30:23.437 --> 00:30:25.253
It was very dank in that sense.

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:31.090
And then we moved to
an apartment with Greg.

00:30:31.090 --> 00:30:34.430
And then the next year
is when Tim moved in too,

00:30:34.430 --> 00:30:37.510
but the apartment was like,

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:39.480
whoa, it's not just one room now.

00:30:39.480 --> 00:30:41.780
We don't just have to
have this little fridge.

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:44.197
I think it was Lily that ask me about it,

00:30:44.197 --> 00:30:45.030
and she was like,

00:30:45.030 --> 00:30:47.517
"What did you have in your
dorm room to cook with?"

00:30:47.517 --> 00:30:50.011
And I was like,--
Toaster oven and microwave.

00:30:50.011 --> 00:30:54.470
We had a dorm fridge with a
microwave sitting on top of it

00:30:54.470 --> 00:30:56.830
and then a toaster oven
sitting on top of that

00:30:56.830 --> 00:30:58.755
the triple threat that's all you needed.

00:30:58.755 --> 00:31:00.470
They should make that as one unit

00:31:00.470 --> 00:31:02.190
that they sell for college kids.

00:31:02.190 --> 00:31:03.210
That's a good idea.

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:04.810
It's probably a huge fire hazard though.

00:31:04.810 --> 00:31:06.300
And also like it'll--

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:07.577
Fridge, microwave, toaster oven.

00:31:07.577 --> 00:31:08.823
Too much amps.

00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:11.990
I'd love to have a fridge

00:31:11.990 --> 00:31:13.810
that you could just put
bread into the side of

00:31:13.810 --> 00:31:16.950
and it pop out toast, that's pretty cool.

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:18.303
It's a toaster fridge, it's a good idea.

00:31:18.303 --> 00:31:19.780
What it is It's a fridge

00:31:19.780 --> 00:31:21.640
that's got a toaster and a microwave.

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:23.780
And when you place your hand

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:26.410
on one of the objects
in the fridge, it knows

00:31:26.410 --> 00:31:28.750
whether to send it to the
toaster oven or microwave.

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:29.630
And then on top of that

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:32.530
it's got like a humidifier
or one of those,

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:34.770
what's those vaporizer
things that our wives

00:31:34.770 --> 00:31:37.340
put around our house
and I'm addicted to it.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:38.703
Yeah, a vaporizer.

00:31:38.703 --> 00:31:40.083
That makes nice smell.

00:31:41.198 --> 00:31:42.130
That seems unnecessary.

00:31:42.130 --> 00:31:44.520
That feels like maybe that
it's in the deluxe package

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:46.050
Deluxe package, man.

00:31:46.050 --> 00:31:47.590
Yeah, you gotta sell them up.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:49.560
We're gonna put that on mythical.com.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:51.530
Take notes, self.

00:31:51.530 --> 00:31:55.660
But we always thought, and
this is, we weren't seeing it

00:31:55.660 --> 00:31:57.840
as a creative space at the
time, we were seeing it

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:01.883
as a space to live in
and express ourselves.

00:32:02.840 --> 00:32:06.300
But we ran coaxial cable to connect

00:32:06.300 --> 00:32:08.690
multiple televisions in multiple rooms

00:32:08.690 --> 00:32:10.560
so that we could have people over

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:15.060
and watch movies in
multiple rooms at once.

00:32:15.060 --> 00:32:18.300
Well, and we had the
Planet of the Apes party,

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:19.623
if I recall correctly.

00:32:20.590 --> 00:32:21.550
I don't know if we've
ever told this story.

00:32:21.550 --> 00:32:22.797
I'm sure we have.

00:32:22.797 --> 00:32:24.920
But we got this idea

00:32:24.920 --> 00:32:26.830
that we were going to invite

00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:31.820
every woman

00:32:31.820 --> 00:32:34.800
every woman that we saw on campus.

00:32:34.800 --> 00:32:36.920
This is sounding like a you and Greg idea.

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:38.500
It was definitely me and Greg.

00:32:38.500 --> 00:32:40.230
I mean I was dating Christie.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:44.740
So I was like, I had to
wash my hands of this plot.

00:32:44.740 --> 00:32:45.580
So Greg and I were like,

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:47.467
All right, what we're gonna do is--

00:32:47.467 --> 00:32:48.300
But I was interested.

00:32:48.300 --> 00:32:51.950
When you see a group of women on campus,

00:32:51.950 --> 00:32:53.357
you just walk up to them and you say,

00:32:53.357 --> 00:32:56.670
"Hey, we're having a
Planet of the Apes party.

00:32:56.670 --> 00:32:59.470
3000 C, Gorman Crossings.

00:32:59.470 --> 00:33:02.713
Friday night, 7 p.m. whatever we said.

00:33:04.250 --> 00:33:06.110
Bring whoever you want.

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:06.943
Yeah.

00:33:06.943 --> 00:33:07.776
Now keep in mind that--

00:33:07.776 --> 00:33:10.500
They're like, Planet of the Apes party?

00:33:10.500 --> 00:33:11.910
It's like, yeah, we're gonna
watch Planet of the Apes.

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:14.180
Don't matter what room
in the house you're in.

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:15.797
You'll be watching it in
sync with every one else.

00:33:15.797 --> 00:33:18.100
'Cause every TV would be
playing Planet of the Apes.

00:33:18.100 --> 00:33:19.360
Now, let me just say a couple of things.

00:33:19.360 --> 00:33:21.613
We're talking about the
original Planet of the Apes,

00:33:21.613 --> 00:33:23.650
because the new ones hadn't come out yet.

00:33:23.650 --> 00:33:24.630
Charlton Heston.

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:27.670
And we weren't,

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:29.270
because we were

00:33:29.270 --> 00:33:31.920
involved in the Campus
Crusade campus ministry,

00:33:31.920 --> 00:33:34.390
as we've talked about extensively.

00:33:34.390 --> 00:33:36.020
We weren't drinking.

00:33:36.020 --> 00:33:37.500
So there was going to be no alcohol.

00:33:37.500 --> 00:33:39.220
But not only was there
going to be no alcohol,

00:33:39.220 --> 00:33:41.110
there was going to be no anything.

00:33:41.110 --> 00:33:43.284
Like no dr--

00:33:43.284 --> 00:33:45.440
And actually, this was
part of the experiment.

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:46.273
Do you remember this?

00:33:46.273 --> 00:33:47.960
I showed up late.
No, no.

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:50.330
I didn't help plan it, no pun intended.

00:33:50.330 --> 00:33:52.873
Me and Greg, it's almost like me and Greg,

00:33:54.390 --> 00:33:57.573
thought that it would be
like a social experiment.

00:33:57.573 --> 00:33:58.406
It's so stupid.

00:33:58.406 --> 00:33:59.770
To not provide food or drink?

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.424
No food, no drink, just
Planet of the Apes.

00:34:05.799 --> 00:34:06.723
That's so stupid.

00:34:06.723 --> 00:34:08.220
Worse party ever.

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:11.160
I mean, if you take care of
the big things at a party,

00:34:11.160 --> 00:34:13.010
the little things take care of themselves.

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:13.843
Yeah.

00:34:13.843 --> 00:34:15.860
Like if you got a bunch of strangers.

00:34:15.860 --> 00:34:17.290
I was going to say a
bunch of strange women,

00:34:17.290 --> 00:34:19.290
but women that you don't know.

00:34:19.290 --> 00:34:20.890
You only invited women

00:34:20.890 --> 00:34:22.200
and then they can invite
anybody they want,.

00:34:22.200 --> 00:34:25.460
'Cause if you invite women,
men will show up, I mean--

00:34:25.460 --> 00:34:26.410
They'll come out of the woodwork

00:34:26.410 --> 00:34:28.620
Well, it because we knew

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.350
that our dude friends are gonna show up.

00:34:31.350 --> 00:34:32.533
But first of all, I don't believe

00:34:32.533 --> 00:34:34.640
that any of the women that we didn't know

00:34:34.640 --> 00:34:36.470
who we just randomly asked on campus came.

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:37.940
I don't believe any of them came.

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:38.773
I think they saw through,

00:34:38.773 --> 00:34:40.910
this isn't a typical
Planet of the Apes party.

00:34:40.910 --> 00:34:41.850
No food, No drink.

00:34:41.850 --> 00:34:43.403
No, you know, no fun.

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:46.750
They probably won't let
us even use the restroom.

00:34:46.750 --> 00:34:48.222
I don't, no one use the restroom.

00:34:48.222 --> 00:34:50.033
No need for it if you're not drinking.

00:34:51.840 --> 00:34:52.673
But it was so--

00:34:52.673 --> 00:34:53.630
There was a lot of people there though.

00:34:53.630 --> 00:34:56.350
You knew that it was kooky.

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:58.700
It was just, I mean, is
that a fair word to say?

00:34:58.700 --> 00:35:01.400
It was just like so, it
was intentionally odd.

00:35:01.400 --> 00:35:02.233
Yeah.

00:35:02.233 --> 00:35:04.660
And so it gave you the confidence.

00:35:04.660 --> 00:35:09.543
If I'm to go up to
strange women on campus,

00:35:10.730 --> 00:35:12.840
because it was just like, this is weird.

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:15.840
So me going up to someone
I don't know is less weird

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:17.690
than what I'm going to
say once I get up there.

00:35:17.690 --> 00:35:18.690
So it's was like,

00:35:18.690 --> 00:35:20.580
you had to work up, once
you worked or the gumption

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:23.160
to have the party, inviting
strangers was easy.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:25.037
It's kind of brilliant.

00:35:25.037 --> 00:35:26.288
Well I'm glad you think that

00:35:26.288 --> 00:35:27.121
because I never thought of it that way.

00:35:27.121 --> 00:35:29.410
But then the execution.

00:35:29.410 --> 00:35:32.340
You had, the whole thing was
a plot to work yourself up

00:35:32.340 --> 00:35:33.700
to talk to strange women.

00:35:33.700 --> 00:35:38.070
But if it had been a good
party, and like memorable.

00:35:38.070 --> 00:35:39.225
It would have happened again.

00:35:39.225 --> 00:35:41.210
There's more Planet of the Apes movies.

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:42.470
Yeah, well, that was the idea.

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:43.750
Let's show the first
one, see what happens.

00:35:43.750 --> 00:35:45.248
I don't recall it ever happening again.

00:35:45.248 --> 00:35:46.273
But it was a disaster.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:48.150
You're not, we're not really talking about

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:50.060
the space beyond the coaxial cables,

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:52.137
going from room to room
and not well hidden.

00:35:52.137 --> 00:35:56.300
But also at that time that our idea

00:35:56.300 --> 00:36:01.300
of what a space was going to
be was so like college dudes.

00:36:02.450 --> 00:36:03.340
It didn't matter

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:05.389
the washer and dryer were in the kitchen.

00:36:05.389 --> 00:36:06.620
In the kitchen, yeah.

00:36:06.620 --> 00:36:08.930
I was doing the laundry when
I saw that giant ass rat

00:36:08.930 --> 00:36:11.020
come out from underneath the refrigerator.

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:12.398
Yeah, very scary.

00:36:12.398 --> 00:36:17.040
But our first workspace was not really

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:21.050
that great of a space because
when after engineering,

00:36:21.050 --> 00:36:23.880
there was the stint
working with Campus Crusade

00:36:23.880 --> 00:36:25.470
at the regional office.

00:36:25.470 --> 00:36:27.010
We were working in cubicles.

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:29.750
We went into a meeting room
and we would set up a camera

00:36:29.750 --> 00:36:32.530
and we would whisper into a microphone.

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:34.120
And those are our first videos.

00:36:34.120 --> 00:36:35.980
But it wasn't our own space.

00:36:35.980 --> 00:36:40.800
It wasn't, and you know, and
so we wanted our own space.

00:36:40.800 --> 00:36:44.490
It was around this time
that, with Campus Crusade,

00:36:44.490 --> 00:36:46.800
since we were, we had special roles.

00:36:46.800 --> 00:36:48.700
It's like we would
travel and we would speak

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:50.990
and we were like, resident videographers

00:36:50.990 --> 00:36:54.110
and all the things we
talked about, like MCs

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:58.130
and sketch artists, comedian guys .

00:36:58.130 --> 00:36:59.353
Sketch artists.

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:01.140
That's a completely different.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:02.643
Yes, not in that way.

00:37:04.583 --> 00:37:08.220
There was a conference that they put on

00:37:08.220 --> 00:37:12.070
just for the creative
types within Campus Crusade

00:37:12.070 --> 00:37:15.033
and we were, and this was
a national conference.

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:20.040
Where they pulled together
everybody from all the regions.

00:37:20.040 --> 00:37:20.873
Creativity Summit.

00:37:20.873 --> 00:37:22.230
Called the Creativity Summit,

00:37:22.230 --> 00:37:25.700
because when you're in a campus ministry,

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:28.430
and or maybe this is true
of any kind of group,

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:30.670
but they like to put on conferences.

00:37:30.670 --> 00:37:31.503
Oh, yeah.

00:37:32.840 --> 00:37:35.520
And we got, it was an invitation only.

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:37.700
Most of the people who
worked in the organization,

00:37:37.700 --> 00:37:39.272
they worked on campus.

00:37:39.272 --> 00:37:41.950
They didn't, they weren't
the creative type,

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:46.950
like a graphic designer, or
a speaker, or a filmmaker,

00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:49.510
videographer type like we were.

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:51.230
So we were invited to this thing.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:53.010
It was in conjunction with a film festival

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:54.200
out here in Los Angeles.

00:37:54.200 --> 00:37:55.033
Du Mar.

00:37:55.033 --> 00:37:59.140
This was our first trip to Los Angeles.

00:37:59.140 --> 00:38:00.900
Super exciting.

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:01.840
I mean, we started meeting

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:03.540
other creative types from other regions.

00:38:03.540 --> 00:38:04.730
It was very cool.

00:38:04.730 --> 00:38:05.720
It was very inspiring.

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:07.360
And then they had speakers to talk about

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:10.580
cultivating and legitimizing
creativity because

00:38:12.070 --> 00:38:14.170
our roles and everyone who was there,

00:38:14.170 --> 00:38:16.680
their roles were kind of on the fringe

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:20.470
of what the main type
of job descriptions were

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:22.000
with the organization.

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:25.430
But I remember one guy
talking and he might have been

00:38:25.430 --> 00:38:26.961
a former like Disney Imagineer.

00:38:26.961 --> 00:38:31.310
He was and he's in that
documentary Imagineering.

00:38:31.310 --> 00:38:32.170
Oh, really?

00:38:32.170 --> 00:38:33.750
Yeah, 'cause he was at Disney forever.

00:38:33.750 --> 00:38:37.350
I remember specifically, they talked about

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:39.480
something that seems so
obvious, and it's exactly

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:43.500
what we're talking about now,
it's the importance of space

00:38:44.753 --> 00:38:47.600
to foster creativity.

00:38:47.600 --> 00:38:50.510
And like I said, even though
we had this instinct of like,

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:52.753
I made hideouts as a kid, and like,

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:57.410
my ex stepdad, Jimmy, we
built that tree house,

00:38:57.410 --> 00:38:58.993
and you and I would hang out back there.

00:38:58.993 --> 00:39:01.720
It's like having your own
space where it's like,

00:39:01.720 --> 00:39:04.775
I can cook things up and
I can do my own thing,

00:39:04.775 --> 00:39:05.608
that was part of who we were.

00:39:05.608 --> 00:39:06.940
We came back from that knowing

00:39:06.940 --> 00:39:10.490
that the cramped little
office that we were

00:39:11.530 --> 00:39:14.300
secluding ourselves in was not adequate

00:39:14.300 --> 00:39:16.300
for the creative ideas
that we had specifically.

00:39:16.300 --> 00:39:19.740
And it gave us permission,
that guy gave us permission

00:39:19.740 --> 00:39:24.740
to want to have a space that
was conducive for creativity.

00:39:25.600 --> 00:39:26.800
Well, and the B-hags

00:39:26.800 --> 00:39:28.210
because he's the one who told us about

00:39:28.210 --> 00:39:30.593
the big hairy, audacious goals.

00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:35.100
Also Scott Derrickson was it
that film festival and spoke.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:36.123
That's right.

00:39:37.020 --> 00:39:38.280
Filmmaker Scott Derrickson.

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:43.090
Yeah, he directed Doctor Strange.

00:39:43.090 --> 00:39:45.900
He directed a lot of horror movies.

00:39:45.900 --> 00:39:48.150
So yeah, so we get back.

00:39:48.150 --> 00:39:51.920
And that's when we know we need a space.

00:39:51.920 --> 00:39:55.560
And I knew that my father in law had this,

00:39:55.560 --> 00:39:58.300
the basement underneath
his, what at the time

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:01.850
was his business headquarters
for his dentistry.

00:40:01.850 --> 00:40:05.030
And you saw this in the L tat video

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:07.420
and we went back to North Carolina,

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:11.090
and you weren't able to go,
but I kind of, it was open.

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:12.320
So I went in there.

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:15.830
But that was the beginning of us

00:40:15.830 --> 00:40:20.830
having a space that was
intentionally a creative space.

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:26.740
And that's where so many of the aesthetics

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:29.290
of early Rhett and Link stuff.

00:40:29.290 --> 00:40:32.250
See a a lot of this has
remained to this day.

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:34.880
The checkered floor, which incidentally,

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:38.670
somebody said on YouTube, that
was like a Illuminati thing.

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:39.700
Oh, give me a break.

00:40:39.700 --> 00:40:40.533
No, no, no.

00:40:40.533 --> 00:40:41.530
I mean, I like playing into it.

00:40:41.530 --> 00:40:42.960
So yeah, it's an Illuminati thing.

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:44.330
That's why we have the checkered floor.

00:40:44.330 --> 00:40:45.550
Give me a break, don't
even think about it.

00:40:45.550 --> 00:40:48.100
The original green, I think about it.

00:40:48.100 --> 00:40:50.930
The original green, Rhett and Link green.

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:52.960
Well, I think we--

00:40:52.960 --> 00:40:54.380
It was really for green screen.

00:40:54.380 --> 00:40:55.570
We painted a wall green screen,

00:40:55.570 --> 00:40:57.300
and then we painted the other wall blue

00:40:57.300 --> 00:40:59.730
just so we could have
a different background.

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:01.280
Because you can do blue screen,
you can do green screen.

00:41:01.280 --> 00:41:02.930
It depends on what color you're wearing.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:03.990
And that's when I started

00:41:03.990 --> 00:41:05.710
collecting those Merle Haggard albums.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:09.133
And we put them around
the front office room.

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:12.540
And since then, everywhere
we've had an office,

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:15.940
we've moved my collection of mural albums.

00:41:15.940 --> 00:41:17.730
They're in the office right there now.

00:41:17.730 --> 00:41:20.570
Well, and there was, it's
funny because there were

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:24.330
basically all the elements

00:41:24.330 --> 00:41:29.330
that we've now always tried
to recreate, were technically,

00:41:29.410 --> 00:41:31.550
if you think about it,
we're in that space, right?

00:41:31.550 --> 00:41:32.650
You've got a workspace

00:41:32.650 --> 00:41:35.960
where me and you have desks together.

00:41:35.960 --> 00:41:38.360
And can develop the ideas.

00:41:38.360 --> 00:41:41.460
We've got a place where we shoot

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:42.610
the things that we're doing.

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:44.130
We've got a studio space,

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:44.963
which it wasn't it was just another room.

00:41:44.963 --> 00:41:49.370
That was the back room with
no windows, which was nice.

00:41:49.370 --> 00:41:52.930
And we had a recording booth, right?

00:41:52.930 --> 00:41:57.343
And because music was such
a big part of everything

00:41:57.343 --> 00:41:59.220
that we were doing at that time,

00:41:59.220 --> 00:42:01.570
so we turned an old closet
into a recording booth.

00:42:01.570 --> 00:42:03.750
It was just nice to be able to go there.

00:42:03.750 --> 00:42:06.600
And if anybody else stepped in, it's like,

00:42:06.600 --> 00:42:08.920
immediately, this is weird, like, what,

00:42:08.920 --> 00:42:11.030
in the middle of Harnett County.

00:42:11.030 --> 00:42:12.810
People would step in.

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:15.400
Some people would show up
and knock on the on the door,

00:42:15.400 --> 00:42:18.163
because it was like a Records Office,

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:20.220
it was a government records office.

00:42:20.220 --> 00:42:22.520
It was right next to the DWI center.

00:42:22.520 --> 00:42:23.353
Right.

00:42:23.353 --> 00:42:25.983
So if someone who had
gotten a DWI had to then,

00:42:26.938 --> 00:42:30.420
the county made them report to this place

00:42:30.420 --> 00:42:31.720
where they would get help.

00:42:32.680 --> 00:42:35.410
And many of them thought
that we were the DWI center.

00:42:35.410 --> 00:42:38.230
We didn't treat anyone, that
would have been irresponsible.

00:42:38.230 --> 00:42:39.670
We would just send them next door.

00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:40.910
Next door, yeah.

00:42:40.910 --> 00:42:44.510
But whenever we stepped in,
I remember this feeling of

00:42:46.060 --> 00:42:47.693
we can do whatever we want.

00:42:48.708 --> 00:42:52.320
It was just this exciting
level of freedom.

00:42:52.320 --> 00:42:54.560
Well, I think there's a pressure
that comes too, I think.

00:42:54.560 --> 00:42:55.630
And an expectation that like,

00:42:55.630 --> 00:42:57.154
all right, we set this up how we want it.

00:42:57.154 --> 00:42:58.190
You set the space up.

00:42:58.190 --> 00:43:00.570
If we can't start making stuff,

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:02.988
if we don't make stuff it's our own fault.

00:43:02.988 --> 00:43:04.830
And we made stuff.
Oh, yeah.

00:43:04.830 --> 00:43:09.830
And I think that that, getting
the space that we needed,

00:43:11.000 --> 00:43:12.765
that was one of the most, I mean,

00:43:12.765 --> 00:43:15.330
not in terms of the amount of content,

00:43:15.330 --> 00:43:18.460
because now we create more
content than we ever did.

00:43:18.460 --> 00:43:20.530
And it's sort of steadily increased.

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:22.000
But that was sort of the beginning of us

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:24.770
being very prolific in
terms of the amount of stuff

00:43:24.770 --> 00:43:26.590
we were working on and creating.

00:43:26.590 --> 00:43:30.760
No one that we saw on YouTube had a space.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:33.460
And everything we shot,
if you watch our videos,

00:43:33.460 --> 00:43:35.720
you would get to know
everything in the space because

00:43:35.720 --> 00:43:38.007
we shot everything there or
on the street outside of it.

00:43:38.007 --> 00:43:41.980
Just look at the Facebook
song music video, you'll see,

00:43:41.980 --> 00:43:44.990
the street corners of
Lillington, North Carolina.

00:43:44.990 --> 00:43:49.800
But on YouTube it was younger
people, in their bedrooms,

00:43:49.800 --> 00:43:53.110
shooting vlogs, shooting stuff like that.

00:43:53.110 --> 00:43:57.050
It was it, no one was doing it.

00:43:57.050 --> 00:44:00.470
No one that I knew of
was set up in that way.

00:44:00.470 --> 00:44:01.970
And I mean, I couldn't imagine that.

00:44:01.970 --> 00:44:03.910
I mean, I have kids at home,

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:05.430
if I was gonna act like a fool,

00:44:05.430 --> 00:44:08.150
I couldn't do it at home
because there's babies crying.

00:44:08.150 --> 00:44:10.780
Well I do think that's a,

00:44:10.780 --> 00:44:13.570
we can jump, we can jump
forward and then jump back.

00:44:13.570 --> 00:44:18.530
But to contrast that, there's
been a few times in our lives

00:44:18.530 --> 00:44:20.990
where we've had to involuntarily

00:44:20.990 --> 00:44:23.620
step back into a restricted space.

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:27.570
And I think that the two times
that I think about most are

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:30.620
well, I guess three times.

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:32.080
Moving to California, right?

00:44:32.080 --> 00:44:33.690
'Cause there was the
studio space in Fuquay,

00:44:33.690 --> 00:44:35.000
that was after the one in Lillington,

00:44:35.000 --> 00:44:36.033
which was another big space.

00:44:36.033 --> 00:44:37.930
That was even bigger.

00:44:37.930 --> 00:44:42.270
But then, and it had high ceilings.

00:44:42.270 --> 00:44:43.840
And no toilets above us.

00:44:43.840 --> 00:44:45.740
But we moved to California.

00:44:45.740 --> 00:44:50.600
We were working with, who
became our good friends,

00:44:50.600 --> 00:44:52.500
Joke and Biagio at Joke Productions

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:54.200
who produce Commercial Kings.

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:56.060
And then we talked them
into letting us have

00:44:56.060 --> 00:44:58.270
a little office in their production studio

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:00.560
because we were just living
in these furnished apartments

00:45:00.560 --> 00:45:02.120
and had nowhere to work.

00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:03.453
But that was a,

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:08.118
again, a constriction on the space

00:45:08.118 --> 00:45:11.700
It was kind of like individual
edit bays in a high rise.

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:12.900
That's exactly what it was.

00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:13.733
Yeah.

00:45:13.733 --> 00:45:18.490
So we were in this
basically windowless room

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:19.670
with a couple of desks

00:45:19.670 --> 00:45:22.000
that like, okay, you come
in and you work on this show

00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:23.790
for a few weeks or months
and then you're gone,

00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:26.210
and someone else will work here next time.

00:45:26.210 --> 00:45:29.980
It was, it wasn't a
lot of character there.

00:45:29.980 --> 00:45:31.830
But you know, that we were--

00:45:31.830 --> 00:45:33.160
We were under a lot of pressure,

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:35.920
But the thing I remember,
the most significant thing

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:38.330
that we created at that time.

00:45:38.330 --> 00:45:40.520
And again, it's just, it's pretty obvious

00:45:40.520 --> 00:45:44.970
looking back that this
constriction of the space

00:45:44.970 --> 00:45:47.540
sort of pressurized.

00:45:47.540 --> 00:45:49.363
Just the location was small.

00:45:50.550 --> 00:45:54.350
And we came up with
the, what did when we--

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:55.183
Caption fail.

00:45:55.183 --> 00:45:57.120
Caption fail, the Caption Fail series,

00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:58.250
which was a little bit of a--

00:45:58.250 --> 00:45:59.410
They were filmed around there, yeah.

00:45:59.410 --> 00:46:02.430
A little resurgence for us on YouTube.

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:07.033
Because we were able to
get a couple out quickly.

00:46:08.660 --> 00:46:10.890
But I think that, there's
no doubt in my mind,

00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:13.410
that that came from being in this place

00:46:13.410 --> 00:46:16.030
like we can't make a music
video really right now.

00:46:16.030 --> 00:46:17.180
The only music video I made was

00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:18.973
we made the Harry Potter song,

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:23.230
which I'm super proud of that song

00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:26.630
because I remember that
we worked all night on it.

00:46:26.630 --> 00:46:27.463
It was one of the few times

00:46:27.463 --> 00:46:29.470
that we worked all night on something

00:46:29.470 --> 00:46:31.720
in order to turn it
around because we needed.

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:35.911
The reason why we made
that song is because

00:46:35.911 --> 00:46:38.540
the last Harry Potter movie was coming out

00:46:38.540 --> 00:46:40.570
and we were trying to
jump on that bandwagon

00:46:40.570 --> 00:46:41.620
and get the views from it,

00:46:41.620 --> 00:46:46.620
in order then to attach an
ad for Commercial Kings,

00:46:46.620 --> 00:46:49.840
so that we can get more
people to watch our TV show.

00:46:49.840 --> 00:46:52.760
And it,

00:46:52.760 --> 00:46:54.623
I mean, it got a lot of views.

00:46:55.550 --> 00:46:57.330
And it was shot right there

00:46:57.330 --> 00:47:00.520
in basically the room
where we were working.

00:47:00.520 --> 00:47:02.170
The ideas fit the setting.

00:47:02.170 --> 00:47:03.520
But when we showed up at that place,

00:47:03.520 --> 00:47:05.600
it was like this is not, I mean.

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:06.433
Wasn't our space.

00:47:06.433 --> 00:47:07.266
It wasn't our space.

00:47:07.266 --> 00:47:09.230
And it really there was like a desperation

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:14.230
of what we were just trying
to find our way again.

00:47:14.880 --> 00:47:16.910
Especially once the show got canned.

00:47:16.910 --> 00:47:21.130
Well, and then once we
decided to, and this is,

00:47:21.130 --> 00:47:24.870
I've never really tracked
the content to the space.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:28.183
But we got our own space again,

00:47:28.183 --> 00:47:30.600
I mean own space, using that very loosely

00:47:30.600 --> 00:47:32.120
because it wasn't anything
compared to what we had

00:47:32.120 --> 00:47:34.460
in North Carolina when we decided that

00:47:34.460 --> 00:47:37.480
the converted garage, or the guest house

00:47:37.480 --> 00:47:40.220
in my backyard would be our new studio.

00:47:40.220 --> 00:47:42.930
And that's where Good
Mythical Morning was born.

00:47:42.930 --> 00:47:45.643
And in a really small,
I mean, I don't know.

00:47:45.643 --> 00:47:47.540
what that room was, and maybe,

00:47:47.540 --> 00:47:50.040
it was a two car garage.

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:52.010
It was the size of this room basically.

00:47:52.010 --> 00:47:55.080
And it was the two of
us and Jason in there,

00:47:55.080 --> 00:47:57.003
working on top of each other.

00:47:58.950 --> 00:48:02.350
And then there was a time,
there was a few months,

00:48:02.350 --> 00:48:05.060
because it was also a guest house.

00:48:05.060 --> 00:48:07.830
Like when people came to stay with us,

00:48:07.830 --> 00:48:09.734
they would stay in there.

00:48:09.734 --> 00:48:11.470
With the relatives and then Antonio.

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:12.303
Antonio.

00:48:12.303 --> 00:48:13.740
When he first moved out,
stayed in there for like,

00:48:13.740 --> 00:48:14.573
a couple of months,

00:48:14.573 --> 00:48:16.895
Did he, he slept on our couch, that couch.

00:48:16.895 --> 00:48:17.990
Yes.

00:48:17.990 --> 00:48:20.143
The couch would lay down like a futon.

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:24.377
But we said like, "Hey, we
gotta, you gotta be out of here

00:48:24.377 --> 00:48:26.217
"before we get to work every morning

00:48:26.217 --> 00:48:28.507
"because there's not
enough room physically

00:48:28.507 --> 00:48:31.960
"for the futon, and you,
and all of us to be here."

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:32.793
Right.

00:48:34.620 --> 00:48:35.690
Yeah, so it was--

00:48:35.690 --> 00:48:36.900
There was a bathroom with a shower,

00:48:36.900 --> 00:48:39.130
so that's why he was able to stay there.

00:48:39.130 --> 00:48:44.130
But we also had the yard too.

00:48:44.150 --> 00:48:47.700
And that's where dope zebra
happened in that backyard,

00:48:47.700 --> 00:48:49.070
several other things that we shot

00:48:49.070 --> 00:48:50.970
happened in that backyard.
The graduation song.

00:48:50.970 --> 00:48:52.690
Because we've been, it's like,

00:48:52.690 --> 00:48:57.373
there's a really good book
by the Duplass brothers.

00:48:58.330 --> 00:48:59.730
I can't remember what it is.

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:02.340
I think we may have wrecked it.

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.660
But basically, they talk about their story

00:49:05.660 --> 00:49:06.680
and their creative process.

00:49:06.680 --> 00:49:08.340
And there's a lot of parallels

00:49:08.340 --> 00:49:11.740
between the way that they
work with each other.

00:49:11.740 --> 00:49:15.520
And the way that we've worked together.

00:49:15.520 --> 00:49:17.140
But they talk about

00:49:18.490 --> 00:49:21.910
the way that they approach
independent filmmaking,

00:49:21.910 --> 00:49:26.870
and it was to find the space for the film,

00:49:26.870 --> 00:49:28.410
to find the location and be like,

00:49:28.410 --> 00:49:30.070
okay, we can go to this town.

00:49:30.070 --> 00:49:33.430
This town has a sheriff's office,

00:49:33.430 --> 00:49:36.890
and a gymnasium, and a library.

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.050
All right, let's write a screenplay

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:42.560
where these locations are featured.

00:49:42.560 --> 00:49:45.270
So they let the space
dictate the screenplay.

00:49:45.270 --> 00:49:46.710
And that's where a lot of their,

00:49:46.710 --> 00:49:48.230
And that's why it was cheap.

00:49:48.230 --> 00:49:50.040
And that's why they were
able to make a lot of money

00:49:50.040 --> 00:49:52.070
off of those, or get going,

00:49:52.070 --> 00:49:53.840
because they could actually make it.

00:49:53.840 --> 00:49:56.800
And that's sort of an extreme example.

00:49:56.800 --> 00:50:00.380
But I think that at each point,

00:50:00.380 --> 00:50:03.293
the constriction of the space,

00:50:04.730 --> 00:50:09.730
or the openness of the space,
has then contributed to

00:50:10.470 --> 00:50:12.170
what we were working on
and what we were doing.

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:13.720
Well, when you talk about
openness, it makes me

00:50:13.720 --> 00:50:17.120
want to skip ahead to this
studio space here, which,

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:19.830
I mean, when you look at
both sides of the studio,

00:50:19.830 --> 00:50:22.940
and we got like, well, not all 80 people

00:50:22.940 --> 00:50:23.773
are working in here,

00:50:23.773 --> 00:50:26.090
and of course, nobody's
working in here now.

00:50:26.090 --> 00:50:29.210
And who knows, but we got this open space

00:50:29.210 --> 00:50:32.200
where most everybody's
working and there's enclaves

00:50:32.200 --> 00:50:34.160
and offices where people can go in to meet

00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:36.050
and have more private conversations,

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:38.850
and we have our office
back here in the back.

00:50:38.850 --> 00:50:43.510
But even this conversations is
helping me really understand

00:50:43.510 --> 00:50:46.053
what we already knew instinctively.

00:50:47.810 --> 00:50:49.500
To really get creative, we needed to

00:50:49.500 --> 00:50:53.600
step out of this space,
because it wasn't doing for us

00:50:53.600 --> 00:50:56.950
what all the previous spaces did.

00:50:56.950 --> 00:50:59.000
And what this space did at first.

00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:00.030
Hmm, yeah.

00:51:00.030 --> 00:51:01.780
Maybe it actually didn't, I mean.

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:04.653
Well, I think that, again,

00:51:05.950 --> 00:51:08.130
this isn't necessarily directly creative,

00:51:08.130 --> 00:51:13.130
but what this space allowed,

00:51:13.150 --> 00:51:16.900
and then what the next door space allowed,

00:51:16.900 --> 00:51:19.110
is, again, I feel like

00:51:19.110 --> 00:51:23.630
the decisions about the space have driven

00:51:23.630 --> 00:51:25.830
the specific decisions about what we do.

00:51:25.830 --> 00:51:27.250
So we got this space.

00:51:27.250 --> 00:51:30.460
We told the story on the
Dell sponsor podcast,

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:32.210
we told the history of the business.

00:51:32.210 --> 00:51:34.837
And we told the story of Ben saying,

00:51:34.837 --> 00:51:36.837
"I know that this place
is beyond our budget,

00:51:36.837 --> 00:51:39.077
"but I want to let you
know that I think that

00:51:39.077 --> 00:51:40.327
"we can take advantage of it.

00:51:40.327 --> 00:51:41.160
"I think that this is the right place.

00:51:41.160 --> 00:51:42.700
"I think we can use it."

00:51:42.700 --> 00:51:45.200
And we took a chance,
and we moved in here,

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:48.160
not having any idea how we
would fill it with a team.

00:51:48.160 --> 00:51:50.860
But again, you naturally fill the space.

00:51:50.860 --> 00:51:54.940
And so yes, we did some
good creative work.

00:51:54.940 --> 00:51:57.590
We've done a lot of good
creative work in this space.

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:00.860
But I think that what this has represented

00:52:00.860 --> 00:52:02.080
more than anything is

00:52:02.080 --> 00:52:04.800
the team building and
the business building.

00:52:04.800 --> 00:52:09.800
And then specifically with the other space

00:52:10.570 --> 00:52:14.860
is what made the acquisition
of Smosh make sense.

00:52:14.860 --> 00:52:18.310
Again, it was like, yes,
we took the other space

00:52:18.310 --> 00:52:19.620
because we could.

00:52:19.620 --> 00:52:20.830
The other half of the building you mean.

00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:22.090
Yeah, the other half of the building,

00:52:22.090 --> 00:52:24.500
because of GMM 22, the season that we did,

00:52:24.500 --> 00:52:26.260
the 22 minute version of GMM,

00:52:26.260 --> 00:52:28.270
we needed more space for more people.

00:52:28.270 --> 00:52:29.450
That only lasted one season,

00:52:29.450 --> 00:52:32.073
but then it was like,
oh, we've got this space.

00:52:32.950 --> 00:52:34.130
And then I think that we were like,

00:52:34.130 --> 00:52:36.430
okay, we've done this business building,

00:52:36.430 --> 00:52:37.943
this team building thing,

00:52:38.890 --> 00:52:43.010
but now we got to get back to what it is

00:52:43.010 --> 00:52:46.240
that we're going to personally create.

00:52:46.240 --> 00:52:48.940
Not just the role, we've
created these products

00:52:48.940 --> 00:52:50.290
like Good Mythical Morning,

00:52:50.290 --> 00:52:52.520
or Ear Biscuits, and we
know what our role is.

00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:55.680
And we come and we sit down
and we fulfill that role.

00:52:55.680 --> 00:52:58.270
But what is the new thing
that we're creating?

00:52:58.270 --> 00:52:59.440
And I think we we had a sense

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:02.560
that it was going to be easier for us

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:03.720
if we created a new space

00:53:03.720 --> 00:53:07.140
that was a trigger for those new ideas.

00:53:07.140 --> 00:53:07.973
And now we find ourselves

00:53:07.973 --> 00:53:10.413
decorating our own offices
for the first time.

00:53:11.550 --> 00:53:14.410
Once we get some furniture
in there, which is happening.

00:53:14.410 --> 00:53:16.250
Do you have any, what do you have any idea

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:17.083
what you're putting in there?

00:53:17.083 --> 00:53:19.370
I put a big shelf up because I'm like,

00:53:19.370 --> 00:53:21.350
I think I want to put stuff on a shelf.

00:53:21.350 --> 00:53:23.840
I think I'm gonna find stuff
that means something to me

00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:24.910
and I'm gonna put it on the shelf.

00:53:24.910 --> 00:53:29.523
Yeah, Jessie's getting some
shelves for some stuff.

00:53:30.640 --> 00:53:32.840
And setting up the records

00:53:32.840 --> 00:53:37.760
and a shout out to my
Instagram for a while back.

00:53:37.760 --> 00:53:38.810
I put the...

00:53:38.810 --> 00:53:40.580
You got to say shout out to LinkNeal

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:43.168
Shout out to LinkNeal on Instagram.

00:53:43.168 --> 00:53:44.001
You got to actually give the plug to it.

00:53:44.001 --> 00:53:45.430
If you wanna look at my record shelf

00:53:45.430 --> 00:53:47.523
that's in the main living room there.

00:53:49.170 --> 00:53:50.680
Makes me happy, makes me happy.

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:52.920
Yeah, well, and I'm, it's been weird

00:53:52.920 --> 00:53:54.820
because this has been such a,

00:53:54.820 --> 00:53:56.560
this has been another
really constricted time.

00:53:56.560 --> 00:53:58.630
So we weren't really, I mean,

00:53:58.630 --> 00:54:00.580
I've been going to the
creative house more than you,

00:54:00.580 --> 00:54:03.040
but it's literally going
over to an empty house.

00:54:03.040 --> 00:54:04.580
I mean, anything that you
saw me shoot over there,

00:54:04.580 --> 00:54:06.980
you see I'm in a chair
in front of a blank wall.

00:54:08.440 --> 00:54:13.440
And I'm also, I value
the creative space a lot.

00:54:14.780 --> 00:54:17.550
But I have a very difficult
time getting motivated

00:54:17.550 --> 00:54:19.673
to actually do the things to have like,

00:54:20.750 --> 00:54:23.810
I don't like, okay,
put the shelf together,

00:54:23.810 --> 00:54:26.460
put this, I don't like doing that.

00:54:26.460 --> 00:54:29.060
I like it when it's done,
but I don't like doing it.

00:54:30.280 --> 00:54:32.810
But I very much look forward
to what it's going to be.

00:54:32.810 --> 00:54:37.550
I mean, we'll talk more about
this in the coming weeks,

00:54:39.910 --> 00:54:41.760
but one of the ways

00:54:41.760 --> 00:54:46.227
that we're exercising
our newfound creativity

00:54:47.430 --> 00:54:50.930
is we're doing original stuff on TikTok.

00:54:50.930 --> 00:54:52.640
That's one of the things that we're doing.

00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:54.873
We're writing these sketches.

00:54:56.080 --> 00:54:58.960
And getting back to this,
there's this interesting thing

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:00.810
that's happening on TikTok, which is,

00:55:02.080 --> 00:55:07.080
it's not a platform that has
a partner program right now.

00:55:07.210 --> 00:55:10.630
It has this early days
of YouTube feel to it,

00:55:10.630 --> 00:55:14.290
where it just feels like
people who see an opportunity

00:55:14.290 --> 00:55:16.290
on this platform and are
figuring out how to build

00:55:16.290 --> 00:55:20.650
an audience and are trying
creative things are doing it.

00:55:20.650 --> 00:55:25.650
And we've always approached
new platforms as,

00:55:26.030 --> 00:55:28.310
for the past few years,
we've approached it more

00:55:28.310 --> 00:55:29.520
as a business or as a brand.

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:33.240
Like what is Mythical going
to do on this platform,

00:55:33.240 --> 00:55:36.010
usually as an extension of
what we're doing on YouTube.

00:55:36.010 --> 00:55:37.530
But for the first time ever, and really,

00:55:37.530 --> 00:55:39.570
this is a very fresh thing for us, I mean,

00:55:39.570 --> 00:55:40.560
we just got a couple of things

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:42.423
that we've written and put up there.

00:55:43.310 --> 00:55:45.650
We're saying like, what are we gonna do?

00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:50.650
Like, what are Rhett and Link
going to create over there?

00:55:50.710 --> 00:55:53.480
And it's totally different.

00:55:53.480 --> 00:55:54.950
It's a different exercise.

00:55:54.950 --> 00:55:57.670
But we're just beginning to be able to,

00:55:57.670 --> 00:55:58.790
be in the house together.

00:55:58.790 --> 00:56:01.173
Sounds like it's gonna
be decorating video.

00:56:02.350 --> 00:56:05.823
Shelf building videos,
bike breaking videos.

00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:08.520
Yeah, well, I'm sure we'll
talk more about that.

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:11.630
But yeah, I mean, it's
rounding out that space

00:56:11.630 --> 00:56:16.250
of what we want it to be
makes it a playground for us

00:56:16.250 --> 00:56:18.043
to then make stuff that we want to make.

00:56:20.210 --> 00:56:22.861
And so it is a hurdle

00:56:22.861 --> 00:56:26.270
that we're starting to clear
and I'm excited about it.

00:56:26.270 --> 00:56:29.240
I'm interested for this space.

00:56:29.240 --> 00:56:32.180
As employees start to come back to work.

00:56:32.180 --> 00:56:34.780
Whenever that starts to happen,
starts working together.

00:56:34.780 --> 00:56:37.040
It's like, what's going to happen

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:39.440
to open floor plans,
are we going to revert?

00:56:39.440 --> 00:56:40.410
Everybody's talking about

00:56:40.410 --> 00:56:43.150
how open floor plans are gonna go away.

00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:48.090
But I mean, the Coronavirus
isn't going to be with us.

00:56:48.090 --> 00:56:49.950
I mean, it may be with us forever,

00:56:49.950 --> 00:56:50.990
but it's not going to be

00:56:50.990 --> 00:56:55.060
the threat that it is even if it comes,

00:56:55.060 --> 00:56:56.510
if it doesn't go away, it's not going

00:56:56.510 --> 00:56:58.810
to be the threat that
it is right now forever.

00:56:59.770 --> 00:57:01.837
I don't know, I think everyone's saying,

00:57:01.837 --> 00:57:06.060
"We're going to reevaluate
the open workspace."

00:57:06.060 --> 00:57:07.560
Because you know what, by the way,

00:57:07.560 --> 00:57:10.050
people don't get any work
done in open workspaces.

00:57:10.050 --> 00:57:12.420
That's one of the things
that I've seen people writing

00:57:12.420 --> 00:57:14.400
is people are distracted,

00:57:14.400 --> 00:57:16.460
and it isn't like
everyone's working together.

00:57:16.460 --> 00:57:19.410
It's everyone's working,
and they're together.

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:23.380
And, I mean, I don't know
what the answer to that is.

00:57:23.380 --> 00:57:27.880
I mean, we've designed this
space, so that it's like, okay.

00:57:27.880 --> 00:57:30.340
And it was kind of as simple as

00:57:31.640 --> 00:57:36.640
which employees might need to
have sensitive conversations

00:57:38.470 --> 00:57:41.410
and/or be on the phone.

00:57:41.410 --> 00:57:42.243
Yeah, but it's--

00:57:42.243 --> 00:57:43.330
And those people get office.

00:57:43.330 --> 00:57:46.470
It is interesting that we built a space

00:57:46.470 --> 00:57:49.310
where people can connect
and be collaborative.

00:57:49.310 --> 00:57:51.520
And that wasn't, that's not how we work.

00:57:51.520 --> 00:57:53.870
I mean, we like to isolate ourselves

00:57:53.870 --> 00:57:55.840
to just the two of us.

00:57:55.840 --> 00:57:57.360
Do you remember the time when we went to--

00:57:57.360 --> 00:57:58.370
We didn't create a bunch of,

00:57:58.370 --> 00:58:00.970
if we would have we created for our team,

00:58:00.970 --> 00:58:03.270
what we created for
ourselves, we would turned

00:58:03.270 --> 00:58:06.460
that open area, not into
desks, but a big living room,

00:58:06.460 --> 00:58:09.140
where people just lay
down on couches and stuff.

00:58:09.140 --> 00:58:10.250
Right

00:58:10.250 --> 00:58:13.670
You remember we went to Rocket Jump?

00:58:13.670 --> 00:58:15.130
Was that Freddie's company?

00:58:15.130 --> 00:58:16.300
Yeah.

00:58:16.300 --> 00:58:18.820
Remember the first time we went there,

00:58:18.820 --> 00:58:21.810
And I was like, where's Freddie's office?

00:58:21.810 --> 00:58:24.970
And he just had a desk

00:58:24.970 --> 00:58:26.790
in the middle of
everything with everybody.

00:58:26.790 --> 00:58:27.623
Yeah.

00:58:27.623 --> 00:58:29.670
And I remember feeling like

00:58:32.020 --> 00:58:33.790
questioning us.

00:58:33.790 --> 00:58:35.650
I was like, are we like--

00:58:35.650 --> 00:58:36.483
Elitist or seperationists?

00:58:36.483 --> 00:58:39.763
Some prima donnas, that we've
got this office in the back?

00:58:41.170 --> 00:58:44.750
It's got a wall of what is now dead plants

00:58:44.750 --> 00:58:46.693
because no one has been
taking care of them.

00:58:48.660 --> 00:58:50.893
But then I was just like, no, I couldn't.

00:58:52.450 --> 00:58:55.360
I understand the idea
of being in the middle

00:58:55.360 --> 00:58:57.200
and being amongst everyone

00:58:57.200 --> 00:58:59.270
but I just wouldn't get anything done.

00:58:59.270 --> 00:59:02.950
And also me and you,
because it's not one person

00:59:02.950 --> 00:59:06.080
it's two people who are constantly talking

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:08.860
through every single
thing that we're doing.

00:59:08.860 --> 00:59:11.200
Sometimes in a heated fashion,

00:59:11.200 --> 00:59:13.943
just whatever the
creative process entails.

00:59:13.943 --> 00:59:15.900
You don't want to watch that.

00:59:15.900 --> 00:59:16.733
Yeah.

00:59:16.733 --> 00:59:17.566
You don't want to try to do other work

00:59:17.566 --> 00:59:19.210
when that's happening
behind you, that's for sure.

00:59:19.210 --> 00:59:21.150
You actually want to

00:59:23.490 --> 00:59:27.110
the idea of what it's like
for us to create together

00:59:27.110 --> 00:59:30.230
is probably better than the idea

00:59:30.230 --> 00:59:32.527
of watching us create together.

00:59:32.527 --> 00:59:34.450
Let's keep the mystery alive

00:59:34.450 --> 00:59:35.950
and see the products on the other side,

00:59:35.950 --> 00:59:39.433
but it gets pretty boring, and
monotonous, and mathematical,

00:59:40.410 --> 00:59:41.740
in the moment, I think.

00:59:41.740 --> 00:59:42.830
And I don't know,

00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:48.210
up until this conversation I
hadn't really thought about,

00:59:48.210 --> 00:59:49.960
the different types of roles here.

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:53.500
And it's like, people are
saying, in general, some people

00:59:53.500 --> 00:59:55.280
are going to are going to
start working from home.

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:56.690
Well that's another thing.

00:59:56.690 --> 00:59:59.570
So it's like, I haven't really,

00:59:59.570 --> 01:00:03.523
we haven't started to
tackle that question for us.

01:00:04.570 --> 01:00:05.690
So I'm not, I mean--

01:00:05.690 --> 01:00:06.930
But it does change the,

01:00:06.930 --> 01:00:08.900
both the fact that people
are working at home,

01:00:08.900 --> 01:00:10.930
but also the fact that there's a change

01:00:10.930 --> 01:00:15.210
in the way people are seeing open spaces,

01:00:15.210 --> 01:00:16.810
that is changing the calculus

01:00:16.810 --> 01:00:20.755
on how long we'll be
in this space, I think.

01:00:20.755 --> 01:00:21.610
Because I think there has been

01:00:21.610 --> 01:00:23.210
a part of this, just like, okay,

01:00:24.810 --> 01:00:26.220
are we gonna need to get another space?

01:00:26.220 --> 01:00:27.610
Are we're gonna need
to get a bigger space?

01:00:27.610 --> 01:00:29.710
I mean, we keep growing.

01:00:29.710 --> 01:00:33.090
But a lot of things
changed this year, I mean,

01:00:33.090 --> 01:00:36.470
yes, there are certain jobs
that can be done from home.

01:00:36.470 --> 01:00:38.600
Some people may actually prefer it.

01:00:38.600 --> 01:00:39.600
There are certainly meetings

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:41.390
that could take place on zoom.

01:00:41.390 --> 01:00:43.270
Does everyone really have to be here

01:00:43.270 --> 01:00:45.720
for a all hands meeting?

01:00:45.720 --> 01:00:48.563
Can some people join by video conference?

01:00:50.148 --> 01:00:53.930
I think that we're going to
be answering those questions.

01:00:53.930 --> 01:00:57.500
I've actually, yeah, and it's premature.

01:00:57.500 --> 01:01:00.530
So I guess the last place I
want to just verbally process

01:01:00.530 --> 01:01:03.423
is on a podcast, but
screw it, let's do it.

01:01:04.488 --> 01:01:07.600
My first reaction,

01:01:07.600 --> 01:01:10.580
like my knee jerk reaction is to be,

01:01:10.580 --> 01:01:12.360
Oh, we got to get back
to the way things work

01:01:12.360 --> 01:01:14.070
because they were working.

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:16.290
But it's interesting that, I don't know,

01:01:16.290 --> 01:01:19.610
I have this as a boss, I
have this defensive stance.

01:01:19.610 --> 01:01:24.093
It's like, well if people
start working from home,

01:01:24.093 --> 01:01:25.670
are they really going
to get their work done?

01:01:25.670 --> 01:01:27.870
And I don't know, I just
found myself thinking that.

01:01:27.870 --> 01:01:30.757
But I know that there's other
ways you can answer that.

01:01:30.757 --> 01:01:32.450
And people also work differently.

01:01:32.450 --> 01:01:33.770
Some people can

01:01:33.770 --> 01:01:36.980
if people work better and
thrive in that environment,

01:01:36.980 --> 01:01:39.360
and there's easy ways to still have

01:01:39.360 --> 01:01:43.060
accountability for your deliverables,

01:01:43.060 --> 01:01:44.920
you know when I'm saying.

01:01:44.920 --> 01:01:46.540
But I also think that

01:01:46.540 --> 01:01:49.690
the work from home thing is complicated.

01:01:49.690 --> 01:01:51.710
I think about my house.
Oh, yeah.

01:01:51.710 --> 01:01:53.130
But a lot of our
employees, they don't have

01:01:53.130 --> 01:01:55.380
two or three kids running around.

01:01:55.380 --> 01:01:56.213
Well, not only that.

01:01:56.213 --> 01:01:57.500
Some of them do, but most of them don't.

01:01:57.500 --> 01:02:01.830
It would be a different
equation if I was a single guy,

01:02:01.830 --> 01:02:05.833
or if Jessie and I just
had Barbara and no kids.

01:02:06.970 --> 01:02:08.010
'Cause we talked about the spaces

01:02:08.010 --> 01:02:09.560
that have meant so much to us.

01:02:09.560 --> 01:02:11.640
It's like, I bet you if I visited

01:02:11.640 --> 01:02:15.627
some of my employees
unannounced, I'd be like,

01:02:15.627 --> 01:02:18.683
"Well they've, this room is a,

01:02:19.657 --> 01:02:21.293
"they've created a creative
space for themselves.

01:02:21.293 --> 01:02:23.610
Well it's funny, I didn't
think about mentioning this.

01:02:23.610 --> 01:02:27.500
But we've been talking about

01:02:27.500 --> 01:02:28.693
how long are we going
to stay in this house?

01:02:28.693 --> 01:02:31.290
Like we're doing that work outside

01:02:31.290 --> 01:02:34.860
and we've had this open
construction pit forever.

01:02:34.860 --> 01:02:37.220
Hopefully it'll be done in a month or two.

01:02:37.220 --> 01:02:39.260
But it's like, how long we're
gonna stay in this house?

01:02:39.260 --> 01:02:41.770
Are we gonna stay here till
Shepherd goes to college?

01:02:41.770 --> 01:02:44.330
That's another at least seven years.

01:02:44.330 --> 01:02:45.800
And I think we're kind
of leaning towards that.

01:02:45.800 --> 01:02:47.410
But Jessie and I have
been talking about like,

01:02:47.410 --> 01:02:51.590
okay, as you get older and
as you live in a space,

01:02:51.590 --> 01:02:53.230
you just start answering
a lot of questions

01:02:53.230 --> 01:02:54.770
like, what do we want in a home?

01:02:54.770 --> 01:02:58.330
But I still don't think we understood

01:02:58.330 --> 01:03:00.810
what we really wanted in a home.

01:03:00.810 --> 01:03:04.630
And I'm very happy with our house.

01:03:04.630 --> 01:03:07.220
But there's a number of
things, like just the idea

01:03:07.220 --> 01:03:10.070
of finding a place, you
can't really get away

01:03:10.070 --> 01:03:12.250
'cause our house is very open.

01:03:12.250 --> 01:03:16.870
And even in this little
guest house, guest room

01:03:16.870 --> 01:03:18.320
that is basically Jessie's office,

01:03:18.320 --> 01:03:19.560
but also our guest bedroom.

01:03:19.560 --> 01:03:21.090
It's very close to everything.

01:03:21.090 --> 01:03:23.150
It's like, everyone who
comes down the stairs,

01:03:23.150 --> 01:03:24.740
every conversation that's in the kitchen.

01:03:24.740 --> 01:03:27.150
And I told Jessie, I was like,

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:31.320
I don't know what our next home will have.

01:03:31.320 --> 01:03:36.320
But, I'll be 50 probably
when we're moving into it,

01:03:36.860 --> 01:03:39.890
and I just have always had this idea

01:03:39.890 --> 01:03:41.790
That when I get to be an old man,

01:03:41.790 --> 01:03:43.680
which I'm approaching that very quickly,

01:03:43.680 --> 01:03:46.740
I just want to have one of those offices

01:03:46.740 --> 01:03:50.160
that's kind of dark,
there's a lot of wood.

01:03:50.160 --> 01:03:52.100
And it's away from everything.

01:03:52.100 --> 01:03:54.080
And maybe there's like
a long hallway to it.

01:03:54.080 --> 01:03:56.920
I mean, they don't make
houses like this anymore.

01:03:56.920 --> 01:03:59.370
But I just have this vision

01:03:59.370 --> 01:04:04.370
of just being able to just
have a retreat in my own home.

01:04:05.650 --> 01:04:07.410
And I probably won't even
need it by that point,

01:04:07.410 --> 01:04:08.432
because my kids won't be there.

01:04:08.432 --> 01:04:11.000
Yeah, you're talking about a panic room.

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:12.630
I'm just talking about
one of those offices

01:04:12.630 --> 01:04:15.110
like you see in a movie,

01:04:15.110 --> 01:04:17.530
with a bunch of books that
you don't read behind you.

01:04:17.530 --> 01:04:18.430
Yeah, okay.

01:04:18.430 --> 01:04:20.760
Space is important,
whether it's a cubicle,

01:04:20.760 --> 01:04:23.833
a little corner of your bedroom,

01:04:24.770 --> 01:04:27.720
where, if you can, it
doesn't have to be big.

01:04:27.720 --> 01:04:29.540
It doesn't have to be like
Rhett's talking about,

01:04:29.540 --> 01:04:31.470
at the end of the hallway,
where you get tired

01:04:31.470 --> 01:04:33.290
by the time you get
there, and so your kids

01:04:33.290 --> 01:04:35.943
will never bother you
with their bike problems.

01:04:36.800 --> 01:04:39.313
I can read between the lines there.

01:04:40.210 --> 01:04:41.750
Hmm, okay.

01:04:41.750 --> 01:04:44.540
I think everybody's got
to have a little nook.

01:04:44.540 --> 01:04:46.520
If you got a little nook

01:04:46.520 --> 01:04:49.200
that's like this is my little space.

01:04:49.200 --> 01:04:53.030
I used to go, in my room I
would go inside my closet,

01:04:53.030 --> 01:04:54.730
and I had a little space in my closet

01:04:54.730 --> 01:04:56.640
where I would just go in there and sit,

01:04:56.640 --> 01:05:00.520
and it just felt like this is my space.

01:05:00.520 --> 01:05:01.860
My closet was too dirty.

01:05:01.860 --> 01:05:03.330
I would go in the attic.

01:05:03.330 --> 01:05:06.020
I had a clean little
space in my closet, man.

01:05:06.020 --> 01:05:09.780
There was a box full of foam.

01:05:09.780 --> 01:05:12.720
I don't know what this
foam was for, or from,

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:14.983
but I would get into
that box full of foam.

01:05:16.610 --> 01:05:17.950
You'd get inside of the box?

01:05:17.950 --> 01:05:19.108
Yeah.

01:05:19.108 --> 01:05:20.340
It was open top.

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:22.913
Yeah, see you're little cozy spot, man.

01:05:24.420 --> 01:05:26.240
If you take anything
from this conversation,

01:05:26.240 --> 01:05:29.390
take that, take finding a little cozy.

01:05:29.390 --> 01:05:31.870
Find your box full of foam.

01:05:31.870 --> 01:05:34.300
No, but I think just practically,

01:05:34.300 --> 01:05:39.300
especially for those of you
who are in a creative field

01:05:41.560 --> 01:05:44.150
I do think that it's important
to just ask the question,

01:05:44.150 --> 01:05:45.573
what is your space?

01:05:46.450 --> 01:05:48.520
How is your space contributing

01:05:48.520 --> 01:05:49.640
to what you're trying to create?

01:05:49.640 --> 01:05:52.360
Because, I mean, we look back

01:05:52.360 --> 01:05:54.210
and we got into a little
bit of the detail,

01:05:54.210 --> 01:05:56.020
but I just think that so much

01:05:56.020 --> 01:05:58.530
of what we happened to
be creating at the time

01:05:58.530 --> 01:06:01.650
was largely dictated by the decision

01:06:01.650 --> 01:06:03.350
that we had made about the space.

01:06:03.350 --> 01:06:05.510
And that's why it's gotten,
it's very important to us,

01:06:05.510 --> 01:06:07.320
and that's why we were quick.

01:06:07.320 --> 01:06:09.090
Once we realized, well not too quick,

01:06:09.090 --> 01:06:11.200
maybe it took a year or two to realize,

01:06:11.200 --> 01:06:14.600
once we realized that this
space had become representative

01:06:14.600 --> 01:06:17.880
of one side of our jobs,

01:06:17.880 --> 01:06:21.490
we needed to push into the other side,

01:06:21.490 --> 01:06:23.440
the thing that drives this whole thing.

01:06:23.440 --> 01:06:27.053
So think about that creative people.

01:06:28.090 --> 01:06:29.550
And I got a rec for you.

01:06:29.550 --> 01:06:32.593
Rec baby, rec baby, one, two, three, four.

01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:39.463
I Highly recommend
watching 13th on Netflix.

01:06:41.030 --> 01:06:45.010
Just to continue the
conversation of racial equality

01:06:45.010 --> 01:06:48.470
and the Black Lives Matter
movement, and again,

01:06:48.470 --> 01:06:53.470
keeping that as an
active part of our lives

01:06:53.470 --> 01:06:54.810
is important to us.

01:06:54.810 --> 01:06:57.560
This is not just, this can't just be

01:06:57.560 --> 01:07:02.410
a flash in the pan for us
personal, is what we've discussed.

01:07:02.410 --> 01:07:04.830
We want to be actively engaged

01:07:04.830 --> 01:07:09.830
as part of the solution
and not bystanders.

01:07:10.740 --> 01:07:14.450
And there's lots of
recommendations floating around.

01:07:14.450 --> 01:07:18.620
This has been one of them that
I watched, this documentary.

01:07:18.620 --> 01:07:19.453
I'll read the summary.

01:07:19.453 --> 01:07:23.167
"Filmmaker Ava DuVernay
explores the history

01:07:23.167 --> 01:07:25.017
"of racial inequality
in the United States.

01:07:25.017 --> 01:07:27.677
"Focusing on the fact
that the nation's prisons

01:07:27.677 --> 01:07:31.107
"are disproportionately filled
with African Americans."

01:07:32.570 --> 01:07:35.730
It was released in October of 2016.

01:07:35.730 --> 01:07:39.860
But it, I didn't know that
when I will watched it.

01:07:39.860 --> 01:07:42.660
And I was like, man,
they got this out quick.

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:46.530
And that's just ignorance,
that I would find myself

01:07:46.530 --> 01:07:50.070
thinking that, it's so
important for this movement now.

01:07:50.070 --> 01:07:53.830
But, I mean, again, it's
like I embarrass myself

01:07:53.830 --> 01:07:57.052
left and right with the
things that I think because

01:07:57.052 --> 01:08:02.052
I just have not been
educated and engaged enough.

01:08:02.330 --> 01:08:04.300
But this documentary is,

01:08:04.300 --> 01:08:08.033
I mean 97% on Rotten
Tomatoes, very well done.

01:08:10.000 --> 01:08:15.000
And it gives you an overview of

01:08:17.298 --> 01:08:19.880
the dark side, the ramifications

01:08:19.880 --> 01:08:23.423
of the criminal justice
system for black Americans.

01:08:24.910 --> 01:08:28.090
And how it's set up, it connects the dots.

01:08:28.090 --> 01:08:30.360
Yeah, 'cause I think a
really important aspect

01:08:30.360 --> 01:08:35.140
of all of this is,

01:08:35.140 --> 01:08:36.790
and this is something that has,

01:08:36.790 --> 01:08:40.410
that I didn't really
understand for a long time,

01:08:40.410 --> 01:08:44.750
and it was reading some black authors

01:08:44.750 --> 01:08:47.253
who explained this to me,

01:08:49.829 --> 01:08:51.903
is just understanding that there,

01:08:53.400 --> 01:08:58.380
yes, in 2020, there continue to be

01:08:58.380 --> 01:09:00.073
a lot of policies,

01:09:01.640 --> 01:09:04.490
whether it's within our
criminal justice system,

01:09:04.490 --> 01:09:06.790
whether it's within our voting system,

01:09:06.790 --> 01:09:10.220
our political system, there are policies

01:09:10.220 --> 01:09:14.460
that if you're me, you didn't
understand why they're racist.

01:09:14.460 --> 01:09:18.880
Right, I mean, you talk about Nixon

01:09:18.880 --> 01:09:22.260
and his, really planting the seeds

01:09:22.260 --> 01:09:24.560
for the war on drugs from the Reagan era.

01:09:24.560 --> 01:09:25.470
And he's talking about

01:09:25.470 --> 01:09:27.520
being the law and order president.

01:09:27.520 --> 01:09:28.550
Right.

01:09:28.550 --> 01:09:32.050
I mean, which is exactly what
Trump is talking about now.

01:09:32.050 --> 01:09:34.153
Yeah, yeah, when you see the history.

01:09:34.153 --> 01:09:37.080
They are intentionally
using the same language

01:09:37.080 --> 01:09:41.400
in order to accomplish what they want.

01:09:41.400 --> 01:09:42.990
Right, and there's a way as a white person

01:09:42.990 --> 01:09:46.550
you think about these things,
'cause I was that white person

01:09:46.550 --> 01:09:47.550
who thought about these things.

01:09:47.550 --> 01:09:52.380
And I was like, well, we talked
about this two years ago.

01:09:52.380 --> 01:09:56.120
It was like, okay, voter
ID laws, that's not racist.

01:09:56.120 --> 01:09:58.490
That's just having somebody,
just requiring somebody

01:09:58.490 --> 01:10:02.090
to have an ID to vote,
how can that be racist?

01:10:02.090 --> 01:10:06.220
But then when you understand
the history of that approach,

01:10:06.220 --> 01:10:09.460
and who is being targeted,
and what is being prevented.

01:10:09.460 --> 01:10:14.070
And it isn't fraud, is actually
certain groups of people

01:10:14.070 --> 01:10:15.700
being prevented from voting.

01:10:15.700 --> 01:10:17.890
And once you understand
the intention behind it,

01:10:17.890 --> 01:10:21.110
you're like, oh, that is
why that's a racist policy.

01:10:21.110 --> 01:10:23.220
When you talk about the war on drugs,

01:10:23.220 --> 01:10:24.890
it's like how could that be bad?

01:10:24.890 --> 01:10:29.410
I mean, how could, just say no, be bad?

01:10:29.410 --> 01:10:32.170
And when you look at
the policies in place,

01:10:32.170 --> 01:10:36.947
for the punishments
associated with crack--

01:10:38.120 --> 01:10:38.953
Versus cocaine.

01:10:38.953 --> 01:10:40.720
Versus cocaine. Yeah.

01:10:40.720 --> 01:10:44.050
Which are the same thing
but they're prevalent

01:10:44.050 --> 01:10:45.300
in different communities,

01:10:45.300 --> 01:10:47.790
white community versus
the black community?

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:51.960
Well, specifically, it was
five grams of crack gave

01:10:51.960 --> 01:10:55.970
the equivalent prison sentence
of 500 grams of cocaine.

01:10:55.970 --> 01:10:57.560
And that was strictly because

01:10:57.560 --> 01:11:00.280
cocaine was a white drug
and crack was a black drug.

01:11:00.280 --> 01:11:02.640
And again, all this stuff was happening

01:11:02.640 --> 01:11:03.610
when we were growing up.

01:11:03.610 --> 01:11:05.950
We were growing up in a
little small town where

01:11:05.950 --> 01:11:08.920
we just thought that everybody's
got equal opportunity.

01:11:08.920 --> 01:11:10.980
I mean, the civil rights
movement happened years ago,

01:11:10.980 --> 01:11:13.130
everybody's, everything's equal now.

01:11:13.130 --> 01:11:14.430
And once you start realizing that

01:11:14.430 --> 01:11:16.710
no, no, no, no, no, no, no, it's not.

01:11:16.710 --> 01:11:18.170
It's not and that's the fight

01:11:18.170 --> 01:11:20.090
that is being fought right now.

01:11:20.090 --> 01:11:24.390
And it is a fight to change policy.

01:11:24.390 --> 01:11:27.680
It is a fight to change racist policy.

01:11:27.680 --> 01:11:31.140
And if you don't think
that racist policy exists,

01:11:31.140 --> 01:11:32.770
then I would, my argument would be

01:11:32.770 --> 01:11:34.160
you're not paying attention.

01:11:34.160 --> 01:11:37.030
And so you got--

01:11:37.030 --> 01:11:38.253
Just watch it, I'm saying watch 13th.

01:11:38.253 --> 01:11:40.800
13th is a great place to start.

01:11:40.800 --> 01:11:41.633
Yeah.

01:11:42.790 --> 01:11:46.060
It lays it all out and
gives you something to,

01:11:46.060 --> 01:11:48.480
you don't have to agree with everything,

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:51.325
to be exposed, to educate
yourself, to be exposed to it

01:11:51.325 --> 01:11:53.510
and then reach your own
conclusions but I think

01:11:53.510 --> 01:11:57.023
it's very well made and it
was very eye opening for me.

01:11:57.990 --> 01:11:59.220
Okay, well we're back.

